00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.309 align:start position:0%
 
you might have noticed there has been  quite a steady stream of policies  announced by all the parties during this  election as you expect  but for voters who will have to make a  choice in just a few weeks time it's  quite tricky to know where exactly the  impact of some of those quite expensive  policies would be what for example are  they going to mean for businesses that  employ people up and down the country  and to try and give us an insight into  that we're joined now by the  director-general of the Confederation of  British industry count that and thank  you very much for being in the studio  with us good morning  today so what do businesses want to see  the next government prioritize well this  is an extraordinary election for  business next year will be the most  important year I think in a generation  because we've seen so much uncertainty  investment plans put on hold so we are  looking from the next government to  really unlock this and I think three  things Sophie are most important first  of all yes there's brexit but we must  get back to the long term challenges  facing our economy around how we build  skills for the future how we do really  tackle climate change and inequality  that massive question of fairness and  this has to be a partnership between  business and government so we're looking  for that a resetting of that partnership  we're also looking to get back to  competitiveness the economy has suffered  through through lack of decision making  but we need to look at the cost base and  we need to look at our immigration  policy to make sure that that works for  business and we do need to build those  infrastructure projects that have been  put on hold like hs2 and Heathrow  absolutely fundamental signal they'll be  open to the world and finally of course  getting the right brexit deal  frictionless trade for for our economy  and also a great deal for our services  so a really important election are  really important set of policies to get  our economy moving again and what do you  make us on the policy announcements so  far and for example this morning you've  laid out what they want to see happen  with immigration so we've seen some just  to take the the Conservatives some good  announcements on digital some good  announcements on investment the  immigration policy I mean I think one of  the things that we absolutely recognize  are the things will need to change a  public trust does have to be rebuilt but  it has to be done in a way that works  for the economy so when we hear told  this way when we hear talk about  brightest and best I think that is they  worry you know if you do want to build  200,000 houses a year you don't just  need the architects and the designers  you need the carpenters you need the  electricians you need the laborers we  need people to come and help us renew  our economy so we'll have to look at the  detail it has to be manageable by small  business the previous visa system was  extremely costly and less than 1% of  businesses were using it so the devil  will be in the detail but it's not just  brightest and best it's people at all  skill levels across our economy that we  need and it's staying with the  Conservatives for the moment as well and  another feature this campaign has been  Boris Johnson ruling out any extension  to the transition if he is Prime  Minister which is not very long to  strike a trade deal to be frank it's a  worry for you well one of the things I  think it's really important to  understand is if you are a business  living through the last three years  these rolling cliff edges of climbing up  a mountain with with stockpiling and  then climbing down again could not be  more damaging so I think one of our  messages to the next government who may  be negotiating at this deal is don't box  yourself in don't set new automations  and  and this time create enough time to  ensure we can get a good deal for our  economy the right kind of deal think so  is it got to send you to Southend do you  worry well we have to watch the detail I  mean I think when we come back in the  New Year and may be in a position of  negotiating a new deal the first it  needs to happen you get the right  architecture with business so that we  have proper evidence-based policy for  that new trade deal and let's make sure  that we don't end up with yet another  cliff edge possibly in June or possibly  at the end of December because every  time we do that we take investment away  from our country and let's move on to  labour shall we and is Jeremy Corbyn a  friend to business well we really  understand the questions that Labour are  asking they are asking questions about  about inequality they are asking  questions about public services these  are the right ones but we look at the  policies on the table and we have real  concerns that they are going to crack  the foundations of our economy you look  at something like the  zatia program the biggest on record the  the fact that we now have a bolt from  the blue  in terms of bt nationalization that will  freeze investment I have talked to  businesses who are already sitting there  thinking and maybe we're next  so we do say again to labor work with  business work out different answers to  these problems but this program that is  appearing to value none of the  contribution that business makes will  simply shut investments out of our cab  what does that mean  freezing investment is that job losses  well what is it well it at the moment it  means some of the really interesting  investments going on hope you've already  heard that in broadband right you know  immediately the announcement had made by  labor some very important investments in  for fibre broadband have been put on on  hold and we need to remember that  investment today is jobs of the future  so absolutely and it's also freezing  those things that we need to create  product productivity in our economy  which has been such a crisis for our  country it's felt like we've had quite a  high spending election campaign so far  I mean austerity is out of the window  you know that the spending caps are well  and truly back on is that a good thing  is that what the economy needs or are  you worried that now you know fiscal  responsibilities a bit of a dirty phrase  well we do need investment it's  absolutely right look at our creaking  infrastructure and you look at the  investment we need in skills but we'd  say do that in partnership with business  and make sure that these plans are  sustainable in terms of our economy we  don't want to get back to a place where  we are overspending so again the  partnership with business you know we've  got some extraordinary success stories  recently looked at renewables energy and  offshore wind prices have come down  there in where's no one thought was  possible that's private sector  investment working with government so be  very careful about excessive spending  pleasures yeah I think it's interesting  listen to hear talk about working with  business because it felt like there was  a time in politics when people who  wanted to be Prime Minister we're really  keen to work with big business Tony  Blair David Cameron and now it's kind of  got a bit out of vogue do you think that  some are almost seeing big businesses  the enemy well I really really  not I mean I think it's been it has been  a challenging period in terms of  business working with government I think  we can't deny that but we have also seen  that in terms of some of these  announcements that we've seen they are  exactly what business needs in terms of  the digital infrastructure and in terms  of investment in skills so that  conversation needs to continue but as I  say what we do hope is that there will  now be a reset that there is an  opportunity I think to do some  extraordinary things if we work more  closely together  climate change is another example but  it's not some would say that big  business perhaps part of the problem and  not the solution  I really don't accept that I think all  businesses are part of the solution our  big businesses many of them are the most  productive in the UK created fantastic  high quality jobs working with small  businesses in their supply chains we  need to work together business is not  perfect we know that there are ways that  they can improve but they our businesses  employ 27 million people they invest  they innovate they are a huge part of  the solution and we need the best  possible partnership of the government  to make that happen okay thank you very  much for being on the program today  appreciate just Caroline programming  from the CBI

